From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 08:57:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 041/184] segacd: read the CDD status the way the BIOS reads it

Every packet failing its checksum, sixteen out of sixteen, while the
drive was visibly answering. Systematic, so not a torn read.

The BIOS takes the ten status bytes as move.l / move.l / move.w -- five
bus cycles, back to back -- and so does the standalone test that went
green on hardware. This driver took them as ten separate byte reads in
a C loop, which is ten bus cycles plus loop overhead and takes many
times longer. The gate array updates those registers on its own clock,
so a read spread that wide straddles an update every single time.

The shape was already written down in docs/cdd-bios-notes.md and
already implemented for the command write. The read was never looked
at.
---
 bios/segacd.c | 26 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index aa62611..fc97f91 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -127,6 +127,8 @@ const UBYTE *segacd_physbase(void)
 #define CDD_CMD_INFO    0x02
 
 static UBYTE cdd_stat[10];
+static ULONG cdd_rawbuf[3];     /* aligned: the read below is long-wide */
+#define cdd_raw ((UBYTE *)cdd_rawbuf)
 static UBYTE cdd_valid;         /* last packet checksummed */
 static UBYTE cdd_score;         /* valid packets in the last 16, latched */
 static UBYTE cdd_count, cdd_good;
@@ -307,8 +309,30 @@ static void cdd_poll(void)
     UBYTE sum = 0;
     int i;
 
+    /* Two longs and a word, exactly as the BIOS does it -- five bus
+     * cycles, back to back.
+     *
+     * This was ten separate byte reads in a C loop, which is ten bus
+     * cycles plus loop overhead and takes many times longer. The gate
+     * array updates these registers on its own clock, so a read spread
+     * over that much longer a window straddles an update *every* time,
+     * not occasionally: sixteen packets out of sixteen failing their
+     * checksum, which is precisely what the console reported while the
+     * drive was visibly answering. The standalone test read them as
+     * long/long/word and passed; this one did not and failed. I wrote
+     * "long, long, word" in the notes, implemented it for the command
+     * write, and never looked at the read. */
+    {
+        volatile ULONG *src = (volatile ULONG *)CDD_STATUS;
+        ULONG *dst = (ULONG *)cdd_raw;
+
+        dst[0] = src[0];
+        dst[1] = src[1];
+        *(UWORD *)(cdd_raw + 8) = *(volatile UWORD *)(CDD_STATUS + 8);
+    }
+
     for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)
-        cdd_stat[i] = (UBYTE)(CDD_STATUS[i] & 0x0F);
+        cdd_stat[i] = (UBYTE)(cdd_raw[i] & 0x0F);
     for (i = 0; i < 9; i++)
         sum = (UBYTE)(sum + cdd_stat[i]);
     cdd_valid = (UBYTE)((((~sum) & 0x0F) == cdd_stat[9]) ? 1 : 0);
